@@ -100,7 +100,7 @@ recorded as a prediction failure (`noPrediction`).
    - A rule by agent (`Rule.Agents`, `agents=codex,sillytavern`) matches the agent the gateway names the request's (`agentOf` in [`gateway.go`](../../internal/gateway/gateway.go): its `magpie-<name>` key, else its User-Agent), in lower case (`cleanRules`, `MatchesBesidesIntent`, `Pauses`). A client magpie doesn't know is named by its User-Agent's product name, before the first `/` or space (`usage.AgentOf`), so a rule can hold SillyTavern or one's own app (raikyou on Discord). The editor's agent picker lists the agents magpie knows, then those `/api/usage?period=30d` heard from that it doesn't (not `other`, nor `magpie`), and takes a name typed in its filter as the gateway reads a User-Agent (`uaName` in [`plugins/routing/client.js`](../../internal/gui/assets/plugins/routing/client.js), through the picker's `field.typed`): "MyApp/2.0 (Linux)" is `myapp`. `routing-rule-agent-typed.test.cjs`.
    - A pause rule (`Rule.Pause`, `pause=<model>`; John on Discord: a model of the group paused in its vendor's peak hours) leaves the model it names out of the group while it holds (`provider.PausedOut`, on `ruleClock`): the group's own rules and each nested group's on the way down to a member, a group named taking all its models, and a model named taking it wherever a group in the group has it (#625). It runs before planning, on every request rather than once a turn, so the model is not first, not a failover, and not kept by affinity, and comes back as soon as the hours end. It holds by hours, days, agents and folders only (`cleanRules` refuses another condition), and is not a send-to rule: `Matches`, `MatchRule`, `ThenUses`, `Ruled` and the compaction rules leave it out. A send-to rule naming a paused model finds it unready and the group routes as without it. The trace keeps what was paused (`GroupRef.Paused`), and the Routing page says so. Every model paused answers 503 (`pausedError`) naming each rule. Embeddings go by the same pauses; manual groups have no rules (`Live`), so none pause there. A pause by folder (#1606, Ti800: a subscription's quota kept for the long task in one folder, `pause=<model> except-folders=~/big-task`) reads the folder only when such a pause is on the way (`provider.PausesByFolder`, then `workDir`): outside its except-folders, which includes a request that says no folder (a subagent's or a small model's that doesn't), the model is left out; with `folders=`, only requests known to be in them. Embeddings and System One's decision calls have no folder. `Paused.Folder`/`ByFolder` carry what was read, and the Routing page says the folder or that none was said. `rules_pause_test.go`, `TestPauseByFolder`, `TestPauseByFolderRule`, `TestParsePauseByFolder`, `routing-pause-folder.test.cjs`.
 3. Candidates come from `planGroup` for a group or `plan` for a provider. They are filtered by `sealedReaders` and `allowedCandidates`. An account past its share is held out of the candidates (`capHeld`): a usage cap's share, each window at its own where the account gives it one (`AccountWindowCaps`, willz on Discord: 50% of the five hours, 40% of the week) and at the account's cap otherwise, or 100% of a window for a Codex account set not to spend its credits (`provider.HoldCaps`, `WindowCaps.Of`, `CodexNoCredits`). A ChatGPT account's call on `codex-auto-review`, which OpenAI counts in no window, is held by no usage cap, only by credits it may not spend (`freeOnChatGPT`, #1593, `TestAccountCapLetsAutoReviewThrough`). The account is held while any window is past its own share, and `cappedError` and the trace name the fullest such window's share and cap. When every candidate is held and one held for its credits spends its resets by itself, `autoReset` spends a reset on it and it is tried (`planned.held`); otherwise a capped or barred set returns `cappedError` or `barredError`. Then `affine` moves the conversation's last answerer first, unless it is resting or nearly used up. An `X-Magpie-Account` header (`AccountHeader`) pins the request to one account: only that account is tried, and when it can't answer the caller is told why.
-4. The candidate loop calls `attempt` on each candidate in turn, through a `holdWriter`. The writer holds the reply until it is clearly passing. A candidate that fails before anything is sent rests (`failure`): out of credit for 30 minutes, out of quota until reset, rate limited until retry-after, otherwise a minute and longer on repeats: doubled each time, up to `longestRetry` (10 minutes) for a failure and `longestRateRest` (30 minutes) for a rate limit back as soon as its rest is over, or up to the provider's own `restCap` in minutes (`Provider.RestCapOr`, at most `MaxQuotaRest`; `magpie provider rest-cap <id> 4h`, #2008: a free key down for hours was asked every 10 minutes). A time the vendor names stands over it as before (`TestOwnRestCap`). The failure count is kept per key or account (`restKey`), and any of its models' answers clears it (`served`). Each model's own count (`restID`) is kept beside it and cleared only by that model's answer (`servedCandidate`): once it is past the account's, a sibling model answered since it last did, so the failure is the model's and it climbs its own ladder and rests alone, its siblings still served (#2241, weizsw: an Antigravity seat's gemini-3.5-flash-lite rested a minute 41 times of 43 while gemini-3.8-flash kept answering; `TestModelLadderClimbsPastSiblingAnswers`). Every model failing with no answer between still climbs the account's ladder and rests the account, and a candidate naming no model has the account's count alone. `rest-cap <id> off` (`provider.RestCapOff`, `restCap: -1`) is for a relay or account pool whose unknown errors come now and then and pass by the next request (Kismet Alan on Discord): a failure that says nothing of why (`failOther`: a 5xx, a dropped connection) then rests nobody and isn't counted, its trace rest `by: "off"` with no time left. A 401 or 403, a model not served, an account's window full, a rate limit (magpie's own 30-minute cap), quota, credit and a restriction still rest it as before (`TestRestCapOff`). The shortest cap otherwise is a minute, the first rest; `rest-cap <id> 30s` is turned away and names `off`. A 400, 403, 404 or 422 that says the model isn't served (`unservedWords`) and says nothing of quota, credit or a rate limit rests only that key's or account's model (`modelRefused`, keyed by `restID`, its backoff counted apart): its other models are still served first in order (#1235, SenseNova's 403 "model is not available in the current token plan"). A Google account's Code Assist (the Antigravity and Gemini CLI built-ins) answering 404 `NOT_FOUND` ("Requested entity was not found.") rests its model the same way, as its endpoint is the same for every model (`codeAssistModelMissing`; Packing1 on Discord: Argon, listed for a Google AI Pro account, answered 404 and rested the whole account; `TestCodeAssistMissingModelRestsTheModel`). A refusal for want of quota, credit or rate, for an account its vendor restricted, or for a verify it asks of its account there, from another computer's magpie (a Remote magpie provider, LAN share) rests only the model it was about (`remoteModelRest`, keyed by `restID`, in `restAfterMarked` and `rateRest`): that magpie serves each of its models from its own providers and rests them itself, so one out of quota there says nothing of the others (bigsteven on Discord: a 429 for one model rested every model on the remote for the 3 hours it named, so a group never moved on to the remote's other models; `TestRemoteMagpieQuotaRestsTheModel`, `TestRemoteMagpieRestrictedRestsTheModel`). Its key refused, the computer unreachable, or the key's own limit there (`keyLimited`'s 429, which carries `X-Magpie-Limit-Reset`, `remoteKeyLimitHeader`), still rests it whole: #2077's reporter had a Claude Code session limit on the other magpie refuse its Qoder models here (`TestRemoteMagpieRestsTheModel`, two gateways over HTTP; `TestRemoteMagpieKeyLimitRestsTheKey`). A 400, 404, 410 or 422 that says the model is retired (`retiredWords`: OpenCode Zen's 410 `ModelDeprecated` "Model exo-free has been deprecated.", whose own model list still names it; MOMO on Discord) rests only that model the same way, is retryable so a group's next member or another provider of it is asked, and is remembered (`provider.Retire`, `retired.json` beside `providers.json`): the model leaves that provider's entries in the catalog (`buildEntries`), so /v1/models, every agent's model list and the found groups go without it, until a request on it is answered again (`Unretire` in `servedCandidate`) or a week has passed. A refresh of the vendor's list doesn't bring it back early, since that list is where it still is. A `provider/model` request for it is still sent. A 400 or 422 that says this upstream's API can't take the request's shape (`shapeWords`: xAI's 422 "unknown item type" (#350), OpenAI's "Unknown parameter", and a limit of a model's own on tool schemas, OpenCode Go's 400 "JSON schema exceeds the maximum nesting depth of 10 levels" on muse-spark-1.3-contributor, #1870), or that it is served on another API than the one asked (`wrongEndpoint`, once the member has tried every API it has a URL for: #2670, a relay with a Chat URL alone refusing tools with reasoning and `none` too), rests nobody: the next candidate is asked once (`shapeRefused`, `failShape`), and the last one's error reaches the agent. A request missing what every API requires ("field required") isn't one, nor is a 200 that mentions it. Command Code's Provider API turning a request away with nothing but a trace id (`unsaidWords`: `{"message":"invalid request error trace_id: <hex>","type":"invalid_request_error"}`, a 400, or a 422 before) is handled the same way: it turns away a request whose history and asked-for output together are over a limit below the model's listed context (the reporter's A/B: one ~748K-token agent conversation answered 200 with `max_completion_tokens` 64000 and this 400 with 384000, the model's listed max output), which another member's model may take, and the key is fine (#2313, `TestGroupFailsOverCommandCodesBare400`). Its overflow, which names the model's context length before the trace id, and an error that names a `param`, aren't one. A relay's own cap on one request's input that says so (`tooLongRe`: B.ai's 400 "Input token exceed the limit", on a 380K request under the model's 1M window; #2445, worksyang) is taken as an overflow (`overflowed`), which no word list of `retryable` matched: a group's next member that may hold the request is asked (`withRoom`, `failOverflow`), nobody rests, another account of the same model isn't asked, and with no member left the agent is told the conversation is too long, so it compacts. A rate limit worded with input tokens (`paceWords`) isn't one, and a plain bad request is still asked of nobody else (`TestRelayInputCapGoesToTheNextMember`). A subscription account that fails any other way with a window full, by magpie's latest reading of its windows, rests until that window renews. A 401 or 403 is the exception: one that names a quota is out of quota already (`usedUpWords`), and one that doesn't is about the key or the request, so it rests on the backoff ladder whatever the reading says (plugins#243, zygs1083-dotcom: a Factory account read at 98% sat out its week over one plain 403; `TestPlain403DoesNotRestUntilTheWindow`). A plain 403 (`plainForbidden`: no quota, credit, rate, verification, restriction or model not served) from a second key or account of the same provider, model and reasoning in one request is the request refused, not each account: neither rests, the first one's rest and counted failure are taken back (`forbiddenAlike`, `unforbid`), and the provider's other mates are asked last (`matesLast`). Before, that one request walked every account of the provider into backoff (`TestPlain403OnTwoMatesRestsNeither`). One account's plain 403 alone still rests it. Both quota rests and window rests are held to `longestQuota` (8 days), so a reset time read wrong (a year off) doesn't bench the account for that long. The next candidate is then tried. A failed provider waits `fallbackCooldown` (1 minute) at the back. A WorkBuddy account the vendor restricted (#1849: 403 `{"code":11140,"msg":"request illegal","displayMsg":{"en":"Your account has been restricted due to unusual activity",…}}`) rests `restrictedRest` (2 hours) as `restricted` (`failRestricted`), not the minutes of an unknown failure: only WorkBuddy lifting the restriction, after the user's appeal, brings it back. Both shapes are known: the built-in's `wbExplain` and the plugin's `explained()` (0.1.17, the mover's `min`) end the error with `provider.WBRestrictedHint` (`restrictedSaid`), and the vendor's own body, its code 11140 or its words (`provider.WBRestricted`), counts only from a WorkBuddy candidate (`restrictedRefused`, `isWorkBuddy`), as another vendor's 11140 is its own. Bare "request illegal" isn't matched. WorkBuddy's 11128 channel refusal stays `promptRefused` and rests nobody. The Routing page names it "account restricted by the vendor", says the appeal is the way back, and gives the account's row a button that lifts the rest (`/api/gateway/unrest`) once the vendor has. Every other rest a key or account takes (quota, rate limit, retry-after, a failure's backoff) can be lifted by hand the same way (#1869): "Try now" on its row on the Routing page and beside its rest note in the provider's key list. It ends the rest only: the backoff count stays, and a refusal after it rests the key again the usual way. The vendor's safety filter refusing a reply rests nobody, whether it refuses the turn before any of the reply is said or once some of it has been said (`refused`, `refusedAfter`). So does one said in a stream a client is answered whole from (a Codex account is streamed upstream for a `stream:false` Responses, Chat or Messages request): each decoder gives the refusal's error event status 400 and its code (`filterRefused`), a whole reply it cut is answered the refusal rather than its words so far, and Anthropic's error shape, which has no code, carries it in `refusedHeader`; before, it was answered 502 `api_error`, asked again, and each account rested (#3274). `restAfterMarked` itself rests nobody, and counts no failure, for a 400 whose error object `policyRefusal` reads as a refusal, by its code, never by words a body may echo of the request. Its note on the try (and in the error the agent gets when nobody is left) is the vendor's reason, with Anthropic's `stop_details` category and explanation when it gave them (`refusedDetail`, #1768: "safety filter: refusal (category cyber)"); none is made up when it gave none. `stop_details` is carried through the IR (`Event.StopDetails`), so a Claude subscription run through the claude CLI and a translated Anthropic route keep it for an Anthropic client too. How many more candidates a refusal before any of the reply is asked of is Settings' `RefusalRetries` (`refusalAsksAgain`, counted per request in the candidate loop; a group's other members count too): unset, every one left (#248); 0, none (#1925, DD on Discord). When nobody is left, an agent on Anthropic's Messages API gets the held refusal itself, the vendor's 200 with `stop_reason` `refusal` and its `stop_details`, streamed or whole (`holdWriter.handBack`), since that is what Claude Code switches to its fallback model on; a 400 is only an API error to it. Codex and Chat clients still get `failTo`'s 400 (`refusedStatus`), which they don't ask again for. `TestRefusalRetries*` in [`refusal_retries_test.go`](../../internal/gateway/refusal_retries_test.go) cover keys, a group and the Claude subscription. The ChatGPT backend's 502 "response protection is unavailable" is about the request's content, not the account: the same history fails the same on each of its accounts and models. It rests nobody, isn't tried again after a moment, and isn't asked of the provider's other accounts or models; another provider left in a group is asked once (`protectionRefused`, `elsewhere`). One cause is known (#1270): a history that replays the backend's own `web_search_call` in a request that doesn't declare the `web_search` tool is refused so, whatever the account, model or turn (a plain turn, a thread's description, a compaction, Codex's own text compaction for a custom provider with `tools: []`). Every request to the ChatGPT backend with such a history therefore declares cached-only search (`{"type":"web_search","external_web_access":false}`, `provider.DeclareSearch`): an account's in `codexBody`, Codex's own sign-in relayed in `codexUpstreamOn`. It goes in the tools, or for Responses Lite (its header, `reasoning.context` `all_turns`, or an `additional_tools` item already there), which refuses a top-level `web_search`, in the `additional_tools` item. A request that offered no tools gets `tool_choice` `none` so the model can't search; one with a forced choice keeps it. A history without a search, or a request that declares search already, is relayed unchanged; Codex's own `web.run` (a function in the `web` namespace, which Codex offers in place of the hosted tool on a Responses Lite model or with its standalone web search on, and runs through `/alpha/search`) counts as declaring it, since the backend refuses a request carrying both `web.run` and the hosted `web_search` (Savin on Discord, Codex 0.163.0-alpha; `TestSearchedConversationUnderWebRun`). siwc, API-key requests to openai.com and the legacy `/responses/compact` are left as they were. Command Code turning a model away for its Zero Data Retention setting (its plugin's switch, 0.1.14, which sends `x-cmd-zdr: 1`; magpie-community/plugins#246) is a refusal of that request's setting, not of the model or the account: the Provider API's 422 with `"code":"cmd_zdr_no_providers"` and the 400 the plugin makes of Go's error line with `"type":"cmd_zdr_no_providers"` (`zdrCoded`, matched as the error's code or type, never by its words, whose "…a different model.","type":"invalid_request_error" read as a model unserved and rested the model on the account). `keepRetry` notes it beside the error (`zdrHeader`), since the error the agent gets may keep no code (Anthropic's shape, or the 400's numeric code). It rests nobody, anywhere (`restAfterMarked` returns `by: "off"`), so the model answers as soon as the switch is off; it isn't asked of the provider's other accounts at that model, which carry the same header (`notMates`), nor again after a moment; another member, the provider's other models among them, is asked once, and with none left the agent gets Command Code's words (`TestCommandCodeZDRRefusalRestsNothing`, `TestZDRRefusalNeverRests`). Another vendor's refusal in the same words, without the code, still rests the model (`TestZDRRefusalIsCommandCodesCodeOnly`).
