t each account: neither rests, the first one's rest and counted failure are taken back (`forbiddenAlike`, `unforbid`), and the provider's other mates are asked last (`matesLast`). Before, that one request walked every account of the provider into backoff (`TestPlain403OnTwoMatesRestsNeither`). One account's plain 403 alone still rests it. Both quota rests and window rests are held to `longestQuota` (8 days), so a reset time read wrong (a year off) doesn't bench the account for that long. The next candidate is then tried. A failed provider waits `fallbackCooldown` (1 minute) at the back. A WorkBuddy account the vendor restricted (#1849: 403 `{"code":11140,"msg":"request illegal","displayMsg":{"en":"Your account has been restricted due to unusual activity",…}}`) rests `restrictedRest` (2 hours) as `restricted` (`failRestricted`), not the minutes of an unknown failure: only WorkBuddy lifting the restriction, after the user's appeal, brings it back. Both shapes are known: the built-in's `wbExplain` and the plugin's `explained()` (0.1.17, the mover's `min`) end the error with `provider.WBRestrictedHint` (`restrictedSaid`), and the vendor's own body, its code 11140 or its words (`provider.WBRestricted`), counts only from a WorkBuddy candidate (`restrictedRefused`, `isWorkBuddy`), as another vendor's 11140 is its own. Bare "request illegal" isn't matched. WorkBuddy's 11128 channel refusal stays `promptRefused` and rests nobody. The Routing page names it "account restricted by the vendor", says the appeal is the way back, and gives the account's row a button that lifts the rest (`/api/gateway/unrest`) once the vendor has. Every other rest a key or account takes (quota, rate limit, retry-after, a failure's backoff) can be lifted by hand the same way (#1869): "Try now" on its row on the Routing page and beside its rest note in the provider's key list. It ends the rest only: the backoff count stays, and a refusal after it rests the key again the usual way. The vendor's safety filter refusing a reply rests nobody, whether it refuses the turn before any of the reply is said or once some of it has been said (`refused`, `refusedAfter`). So does one said in a stream a client is answered whole from (a Codex account is streamed upstream for a `stream:false` Responses, Chat or Messages request): each decoder gives the refusal's error event status 400 and its code (`filterRefused`), a whole reply it cut is answered the refusal rather than its words so far, and Anthropic's error shape, which has no code, carries it in `refusedHeader`; before, it was answered 502 `api_error`, asked again, and each account rested (#3274). `restAfterMarked` itself rests nobody, and counts no failure, for a 400 whose error object `policyRefusal` reads as a refusal, by its code, never by words a body may echo of the request. Its note on the try (and in the error the agent gets when nobody is left) is the vendor's reason, with Anthropic's `stop_details` category and explanation when it gave them (`refusedDetail`, #1768: "safety filter: refusal (category cyber)"); none is made up when it gave none. `stop_details` is carried through the IR (`Event.StopDetails`), so a Claude subscription run through the claude CLI and a translated Anthropic route keep it for an Anthropic client too. How many more candidates a refusal before any of the reply is asked of is Settings' `RefusalRetries` (`refusalAsksAgain`, counted per request in the candidate loop; a group's other members count too): unset, every one left (#248); 0, none (#1925, DD on Discord). When nobody is left, an agent on Anthropic's Messages API gets the held refusal itself, the vendor's 200 with `stop_reason` `refusal` and its `stop_details`, streamed or whole (`holdWriter.handBack`), since that is what Claude Code switches to its fallback model on; a 400 is only an API error to it. Codex and Chat clients still get `failTo`'s 400 (`refusedStatus`), which they don't ask again for. `TestRefusalRetries*` in [`refusal_retries_test.go`](../../internal/gateway/refusal_retries_test.go) cover keys, a group and the Claude subscription. The ChatGPT backend's 502 "response protection is unavailable" is about the request's content, not the account: the same history fails the same on each of its accounts and models. It rests nobody, isn't tried again after a moment, and isn't asked of the provider's other accounts or models; another provider left in a group is asked once (`protectionRefused`, `elsewhere`). One cause is known (#1270): a history that replays the backend's own `web_search_call` in a request that doesn't declare the `web_search` tool is refused so, whatever the account, model or turn (a plain turn, a thread's description, a compaction, Codex's own text compaction for a custom provider with `tools: []`). Every request to the ChatGPT backend with such a history therefore declares cached-only search (`{"type":"web_search","external_web_access":false}`, `provider.DeclareSearch`): an account's in `codexBody`, Codex's own sign-in relayed in `codexUpstreamOn`. It goes in the tools, or for Responses Lite (its header, `reasoning.context` `all_turns`, or an `additional_tools` item already there), which refuses a top-level `web_search`, in the `additional_tools` item. A request that offered no tools gets `tool_choice` `none` so the model can't search; one with a forced choice keeps it. A history without a search, or a request that declares search already, is relayed unchanged; Codex's own `web.run` (a function in the `web` namespace, which Codex offers in place of the hosted tool on a Responses Lite model or with its standalone web search on, and runs through `/alpha/search`) counts as declaring it, since the backend refuses a request carrying both `web.run` and the hosted `web_search` (Savin on Discord, Codex 0.163.0-alpha; `TestSearchedConversationUnderWebRun`). siwc, API-key requests to openai.com and the legacy `/responses/compact` are left as they were. Command Code turning a model away for its Zero Data Retention setting (its plugin's switch, 0.1.14, which sends `x-cmd-zdr: 1`; magpie-community/plugins#246) is a refusal of that request's setting, not of the model or the account: the Provider API's 422 with `"code":"cmd_zdr_no_providers"` and the 400 the plugin makes of Go's error line with `"type":"cmd_zdr_no_providers"` (`zdrCoded`, matched as the error's code or type, never by its words, whose "…a different model.","type":"invalid_request_error" read as a model unserved and rested the model on the account). `keepRetry` notes it beside the error (`zdrHeader`), since the error the agent gets may keep no code (Anthropic's shape, or the 400's numeric code). It rests nobody, anywhere (`restAfterMarked` returns `by: "off"`), so the model answers as soon as the switch is off; it isn't asked of the provider's other accounts at that model, which carry the same header (`notMates`), nor again after a moment; another member, the provider's other models among them, is asked once, and with none left the agent gets Command Code's words (`TestCommandCodeZDRRefusalRestsNothing`, `TestZDRRefusalNeverRests`). Another vendor's refusal in the same words, without the code, still rests the model (`TestZDRRefusalIsCommandCodesCodeOnly`).
 5. The last candidate's error reaches the agent, with one exception for a group (#1418, `patience.go`). When every member has failed in a way that passes (`busy`: 408, 500, 502, 503, 504 or 529 that isn't a proxy, a quota, response protection, or an upstream that answered a stream request with a whole reply, which fails the same however often it is asked (`didNotStream`; the same-one retries of `passing` leave it alone too, `TestStreamlessMemberIsAskedOnce`), or a 429 that is a short rate limit, not a plan or credit), and nothing of the reply has been sent, the members that failed so are asked again in turn after a pause (`replan`). The pause is `retryPause` doubled each round up to `replanLongest()` (30 times `retryPause`, 30s), and never shorter than the soonest Retry-After; a member whose Retry-After ends past the pause is asked after the others. This goes on while the group's `Patience` lasts. A member of a group in the group uses the innermost that sets `Patience` (`innerPatience`, #2410), as FirstToken (#2246) and Sink (#2255) do; unset falls back to the group asked for, and an inner `PatienceOff` skips replan for that member (`TestNestedGroupPatienceThroughOuter`, `TestNestedGroupPatienceInnerOff`, `TestNestedGroupPatienceFallsBackToOuter`). `replanRounds` (12) is only a backstop that never comes first: the pauses alone add up to `PatienceLongest` in 11 rounds (with 6, a group set to 240s gave up after 90s of pauses, real-jacket on #1418; `TestPatienceIsUsedUp`). The budget is the group's `Patience`, counted from the first time every member had failed, when the agent would have had the error (`waitFrom`; `Group.Waits`: 0 is `PatienceDefault`, 60s; -1, `PatienceOff`, turns it off; at most `PatienceLongest`, 240s). A pause that would end past the budget isn't taken, and the agent gets the error. While another member is busy too, the last one isn't first tried again alone (`passing`): the members take turns, and once the budget has begun, the same-one retries aren't taken either. While the budget lasts, the last candidate's failure is held (`newHoldWriter`), and a streaming agent is kept alive through the pause with SSE comments (`pauseAlive`). The try is traced with `Again` (the pause), `Replan` (how many are asked again) and `Patience` (the budget in seconds). The group editor's "Every member busy" row sets it. A plain provider keeps only the same-one retries `passing` gives the last one left. An agent that goes away in either pause (the same-one retry's or a group's replan) ends the request there with 499: nobody is asked again, and the try whose pause it was is marked `Gone` (#1894, heng91: a relay down while its proxy was off, the agent's window closed). The Routing page says the agent canceled the request before the retry and lists it as canceled, rather than ending its story on "tried again in 2s", a retry that never came (`TestAgentGoneInTheRetryPause`, `routing-agent-gone.test.cjs`). A try that answered is recorded (`served`, `answered`) for routing, affinity and usage. A try is added to the route's `Tries` only as it is asked, so a later try is the record that the gateway went on from this one, and the Routing page tells what came of a try from that, never from where it stood in the order (#2281, xishuchkui-code: an Antigravity 502 empty reply that went on to the next account was told as "an error another account wouldn't fix, so Claude Code gets it"). The one case the record can't show is the last try left out of its allowance after an earlier failure that wasn't one, where the agent is told the earlier error: that try is marked `Earlier` (`routing-went-on.test.cjs`).
 
